<div class="note"><b class="h">Local claim risks</b><ul class="chips" style="margin:10px 0 0"><li>Falling firs</li><li>Ice storms</li><li>High-value interiors</li><li>Water leaks</li></ul></div>

<h2>Homes and trees in Lake Oswego</h2>
<p>Lake Oswego lies mostly in Clackamas County, with small parts in Multnomah County and Washington County. The city wraps around Oswego Lake, and neighborhoods like First Addition, Palisades, Forest Highlands and Mountain Park sit under a dense canopy of Douglas fir and other tall trees. Homes range from mid-century houses to large custom homes with high-end interiors.</p>
<p>Higher-value homes bring higher-value claims: custom millwork, stone, specialty flooring and complex roofs that a standard estimate doesn’t price well.</p>
<h2>Storm history</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The February 2021 ice storm</strong> was especially damaging in Clackamas County, with many large trees down and long power outages.</li>
<li><strong>Winter windstorms</strong> regularly bring down limbs and whole trees on saturated soil.</li>
<li><strong>Heat and drought</strong> in recent summers have stressed trees, which can make them more likely to fail in later storms.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What we focus on</h2>
<p>Structural damage from tree impacts, matching of high-end finishes, crane and removal costs, and interior water damage that spreads into custom cabinetry and flooring. We document these thoroughly so nothing is valued as standard grade when it isn’t.</p>

<h2>Neighborhoods we cover</h2>
<p>We inspect homes and buildings throughout Lake Oswego, including First Addition, Lake Grove, Westlake, Palisades, Forest Highlands and Mountain Park, and the surrounding parts of Clackamas County.</p>
<h2>Services available in Lake Oswego</h2>
<p>Every Oregon Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in Lake Oswego, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/property-appraisals/appraisal-clause-help/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/home-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/roof-hail-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">Windstorm &amp; Tornado Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/ice-storm-snow-appraisal/">Ice Storm &amp; Snow Load Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisals/house-fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Lake Oswego property owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My home has custom finishes. Will the insurer price them correctly?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often not at first. Estimates tend to default to standard materials. We document and price the actual grade of finishes.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays to remove a fir that fell on my house?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies pay to remove a tree that damaged a covered structure, up to a limit. Emergency removal to protect the home may be handled separately.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you cover the rest of Clackamas County?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, including West Linn, Oregon City, Happy Valley, Milwaukie and rural areas.</p></div></details></div>
